WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.783
Authentication middleware should provide
one clear

2
00:00:02.783 --> 00:00:05.257
entry point into protected Express routes.

3
00:00:05.257 --> 00:00:07.921
Extract a token from the expected header

4
00:00:07.921 --> 00:00:10.775
or cookie transport, reject ambiguous
credentials, and

5
00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:13.630
enforce configured size limits before
expensive processing.

6
00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.674
Await full verification and claim
validation before setting

7
00:00:16.674 --> 00:00:19.909
request authentication state or continuing
to the route.

8
00:00:19.909 --> 00:00:23.524
A verified payload is still an external
data structure.

9
00:00:23.524 --> 00:00:27.140
TypeScript interfaces and casts do not
check runtime values.

10
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.375
If the application relies on subject,
tenant, or

11
00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:34.370
scopes, validate their presence, types,
bounds, and issuer-defined meaning.

12
00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.415
A scope claim may be a space-delimited
string

13
00:00:37.415 --> 00:00:41.030
under one profile and a different
structure under another.

14
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.314
Accept the documented shape and normalize

15
00:00:43.314 --> 00:00:46.263
it deliberately, rather than coercing
arbitrary values.

16
00:00:46.263 --> 00:00:50.164
Map accepted values into a small context,
such as subject

17
00:00:50.164 --> 00:00:53.970
identifier, tenant identifier, and a
readonly list of scopes.

18
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.109
Include issuer identity when supporting
multiple issuers, since

19
00:00:57.109 --> 00:00:59.964
a subject need not be globally unique.

20
00:00:59.964 --> 00:01:04.340
Do not cast the entire payload to the
application's User model.

21
00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.480
A token generally cannot guarantee every
field, current

22
00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.190
account condition, or relationship that a
domain object represents.

23
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.474
The example shows a runtime validator

24
00:01:13.474 --> 00:01:15.567
after cryptographic and registered-claim
validation.

25
00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:19.943
Its output is the only value assigned to
the authenticated context.

26
00:01:19.943 --> 00:01:22.227
Route code consumes that context instead

27
00:01:22.227 --> 00:01:25.081
of repeatedly parsing the raw bearer
token.

28
00:01:25.081 --> 00:01:28.221
Copy only required fields, and avoid
retaining the

29
00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:31.836
token or arbitrary claim objects in
request-scoped diagnostic data.

30
00:01:31.836 --> 00:01:34.500
A readonly type documents intent but does

31
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.354
not itself enforce a runtime security
boundary.

32
00:01:37.354 --> 00:01:40.970
Middleware order must make failure
terminal for protected access.

33
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.300
Missing credentials, rejected signatures,
invalid claims, or unavailable

34
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:47.534
verification keys must prevent the handler
from running.

35
00:01:47.534 --> 00:01:49.818
Return an appropriate authentication
response for

36
00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:52.196
invalid credentials, and distinguish
infrastructure outages

37
00:01:52.196 --> 00:01:54.670
in sanitized operational reporting where
useful.

38
00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:57.144
Neither case creates an authenticated
context.

39
00:01:57.144 --> 00:02:01.045
Place authorization after authentication,
and ensure asynchronous failures reach an

40
00:02:01.045 --> 00:02:04.660
error handler that cannot accidentally
resume the protected route.

